1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 g6 6. Be3 Bg7 7. Be2 Nc6 8.
O-O O-O 9. Nb3 Be6 10. f4 Na5 11. f5 Bc4 12. g4 Rc8 {"Giving me the chance
once again to play a variation I discovered in 1950."} (12... Nd7 {[illegible
note about this having been played in the Scottish Championship 1960, 1961 or
1962]}) (12... d5 13. g5 {Aitken-B.H.Wood, British Championship 1954 (1-0)}) (
12... Nd7 13. Nxa5 Bxe2 14. Qxe2 Qxa5 15. Nd5 Rfe8 16. Qf2 Ne5 17. fxg6 hxg6
18. Bd4 f6 19. a3 Qd8 20. Rae1 b6 21. g5 Rf8 22. Qh4 fxg5 23. Qxg5 Nf3+ 24.
Rxf3 Rxf3 25. Nxe7+ Kf7 26. Qxg6+ Kxe7 27. Qxg7+ Rf7 28. Bf6+ Ke8 29. Qg8+ {
1-0, Mott-Probst, corr 1961}) (12... Bxe2 13. Qxe2 {Aitken-Panagopoulos,
Munich ol 1958 (½-½)}) 13. e5 $146 {"This is my new move. I discovered it
over the board in a game I played against W. Winter..." (Aitken)} (13. g5 Nd7
14. Nxa5 Qxa5 15. f6 exf6 16. Bxc4 Rxc4 17. Qxd6 Ne5 18. Nd5 fxg5 19. Bxg5 Rxc2
20. Bf6 Ng4 21. b4 Qa6 22. Ne7+ Kh8 23. Nxg6+ Kg8 24. Ne7+ Kh8 25. Ng6+ Kg8 {
½-½, Milner-Barry-Winter, British Championship 1946}) 13... dxe5 {"Played
after long consideration and an unpleasant surprise to me. It says a lot for
Footner that, faced with a prepared variation, he found what both Winter and
myself had overlooked—that Black can and should sacrifice the piece. It is
clear after move 14 that White is in great danger." (Aitken)} (13... Bxe2 14.
Qxe2 dxe5 15. Rad1 Qc7 16. g5 Nh5 17. Nd5 Qc4 {"Now I played} 18. Qxc4 {
but though I won material Winter fought very hard and eventually drew." 
(Aitken-Winter, Lud-Eagle club tournament 1950)} (18. Nxe7+ $1 Kh8 19. Qg2 $18
{Houdini 4 Pro}) ({"Later I found the improvement} 18. Qg2 Nc6 19. f6 exf6 20.
gxf6 Bh8 21. Na5 {and wins" (Aitken)})) 14. Qxd8 Rfxd8 15. Nxa5 Bxe2 16. Nxe2
Rxc2 17. Rfd1 {Source: bulletin; BCM, October 1962, p289; Glasgow Herald, 5
November 1962} (17. Rfd1 {BCM gives the additional move} Rxe2 {as having been
played, but Aitken's own notes quoted in the Glasgow Herald make it clear that
no move was played: "Black's resignation is due to a tragic fingerslip. He
meant to play 17...Rxd1+ 18.Rxd1 Rxe2 but in his excitement picked up my
knight first and immediately resigned on realising what he had done."} ({
"But if he had played as he had intended (} 17... Rxd1+ 18. Rxd1 Rxe2 {) White
still wins by} ({"Unfortunately, this is not the end of the story. As Golombek
pointed out later, Black could play} 18... Nxg4 $1 {when White has no adequate
line as after} 19. Rd8+ Bf8 20. f6 ({Houdini 4 Pro:} 20. Bd2 $1 Kg7 21. fxg6
hxg6 22. h3 Nf6 23. Nc3 $11) 20... exf6 21. Bxa7 Kg7 22. h3 Rxe2 23. hxg4 Rxb2
{, Black's pawn mass is overwhelming. So in the final analysis the interesting
13.e5 is unsound." (Aitken)}) 19. Rd8+ Bf8 20. Bh6 Nd7 21. g5 {for if} Rd2 22.
Bxf8 {- a curious turn. This was the point (? illegible] of my 17th move and
it is some satisfaction to know that I did not win merely by a fingerslip." 
(Aitken)})) 1-0

